The reference trail resolves the intended variant: `MARGIN_Q` changes only the solve target from Γ to Γ/q; I will use that exact `margin_batch.py` implementation, not the later “cleared” numerical refinement, because the standing harness also registers `MARGIN_Q` against this file. One provenance caveat is now explicit: elapsed-time and source-hash fields make whole receipt files inherently non-reproducible byte-for-byte, so the compatibility gate will hash and compare every deterministic evaluation field (world identity, seeds, search path/certificates, configurations, bits, joules, served counts, and summaries) in canonical JSON and will separately identify the excluded runtime/provenance envelope.
codex
Ranked hypotheses for the compatibility/result checks:

1. If the only operative change is Γ→Γ/q, setting the divisor to one will reproduce all deterministic sealed receipt fields exactly; any mismatch would falsify implementation compatibility.
2. If the service defect is driven by provisioning at Γ and judging mode eligibility at qΓ, `MARGIN_Q` will sharply reduce “no eligible mode” events and restore rate-target attainment without changing tapes, seeds, caps, or selector rules.
3. If the prior ~6% gap largely exploited that defect, the corrected date gains will cluster near zero, may turn negative on some dates, and their ordering versus the sealed gains will weaken; the paired date analysis will test that directly.

I’m proceeding with those fixed predictions; no input or acceptance rule will be tuned from outcomes.
